# pulse starter: webhook-relay ({{.Lang}})

The most common serverless pattern in the wild: receive webhooks
(Stripe, GitHub, anything), **ack instantly**, process reliably in the
background — with automatic retries and a dead-letter queue for the ones
that keep failing. No table needed; `resources` holds only the queues.

```
services/receive-webhook/   POST /webhooks   → 202 immediately + queues the payload
services/process-webhook/   webhooks queue   → does the real work; raising = retry
```

```
sender ──▶ POST /webhooks (202 in ms) ──▶ webhooks queue ──▶ processWebhook
                                                │ 3 failures, 5s apart
                                                └──────────▶ webhooks-dlq
```

Try the happy path:

```bash
pulse start
curl -X POST localhost:3000/webhooks -d '{"type":"invoice.paid","amount":42}'
# → 202 instantly; a moment later the console shows:
#   processWebhook | processed webhook <id> · type invoice.paid (attempt 1)
```

And the failure path — watch three attempts, then the dead-letter queue:

```bash
curl -X POST localhost:3000/webhooks -d '{"type":"bad.event","fail":true}'
# processWebhook ! ... failed on purpose (attempt 1) …then 2, then 3, 5s apart
# ☠ webhooks: message moved to dead-letter queue webhooks-dlq after 3 receives
pulse list        # shows the dlq depth
```

To test the worker alone: `pulse invoke processWebhook -e events/sqs-webhook.json`.

pulse points the vanilla AWS SDK at its local mocks via `AWS_ENDPOINT_URL`,
so this code runs unmodified in real AWS.

Prefer the other language? `pulse init <name> --template webhook-relay --lang node|python`
